        CountDownLatch ready = new CountDownLatch(threads);
        CountDownLatch start = new CountDownLatch(1);
        List<Future<ScheduleService.StoredResponse>> futures = new ArrayList<>();
        // also capture exceptions
        for (int i = 0; i < threads; i++) {
            int idx = i;
            futures.add(pool.submit(() -> {
                ready.countDown();
                start.await();
                try {
                    return scheduleService.submit(new ScheduleDtos.SubmitRequest("REQ-C-" + idx, "SAT-A", "GS-BJ", "W0001", 3));
                } catch (ApiException e) {
                    return new ScheduleService.StoredResponse(e.getStatus(), e.getCode());
                }
            }));
        }
        assertTrue(ready.await(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS));
        start.countDown();
        int ok = 0, conflict = 0;
        for (Future<...> f : futures) {
            StoredResponse r = f.get(15, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
            if (r.httpStatus() == 200) ok++; else if (r.httpStatus() == 409) conflict++;
        }
        pool.shutdownNow();
        assertEquals(1, ok);
        assertEquals(threads - 1, conflict);
        Integer entries = jdbc.queryForObject("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM schedule_entry WHERE status='SCHEDULED'", Integer.class);
        assertEquals(1, entries);
    }
```

Wait — all threads use same window W0001 with different requestIds. The winner inserts entry; losers: findConflictingEntries — do they see the winner's entry? Only if winner committed before losers' SELECT. Race: all select (see nothing), all try insert → unique constraint serializes → one wins, others get DuplicateKeyException → 409 CONCURRENT_CONFLICT. Alternatively some losers see winner's committed entry → WINDOW_CONFLICT 409 stored. Either way exactly one 200. But careful: losers that get WINDOW_CONFLICT store a schedule_request row with 409 — fine.

But there's a deadlock risk: tx A inserted entry (uncommitted), tx B inserts same active_slot → blocks. A then inserts schedule_request, commits. B unblocks → duplicate key. No deadlock since B only waits on A's lock and A doesn't wait on B. With 8 threads, H2 MVStore locking handles it. Lock timeout 10s might be tight if serialization is slow, but inserts are fast. OK.

